## Progress audit, 14 September 2026

Historical conception notes and a CRS/disbursement figure exist, but they are not
the new comparative result. The September 13 programme decision supersedes their
disbursement-only framing and asserted instrument-substitution conclusion.
No new comparable lifecycle figure or LaTeX manuscript is delivered yet; 0711,
0729 and 0730 remain the relevant dependencies (0729 through 0730).

Next manuscript work follows the 0729 feasibility memo: state the supported
estimand and population, then use the 0730 analysis for the central figure. Do not
relabel the MVP's approval-to-closing histogram as a JETP acceleration comparison.
Retain the old figure only as explicitly historical material unless it separately
passes the common-clock/common-denominator acceptance check.

## Causal manuscript acceptance, 14 September 2026

The introduction must state the intervention and target population; the methods
must explain why the comparison approximates the untreated counterfactual, not
merely name an estimator. Distinguish the total partnership effect from a selected
project-support effect. Use a broader comparable-country pool only as supported by
the 0729/0730 design, with the relevant pre/post outcomes and assignment assumptions.

The central figure should display the primary effect and uncertainty at supported
early-stage horizons, or the corresponding longitudinal contrast where the design
requires it. Include cohort/risk-set support and a companion identification
diagnostic. Fix the figure form after the design decision, not before it.

First additional acceptance check: a reader can trace each causal sentence to the
estimand, identifying assumption, evidence supporting that assumption and its
sensitivity result. A descriptive stage gap presented as an effect must fail.

- [ ] Identification argument and most serious alternative explanation are explicit
- [ ] Main figure, uncertainty and robustness correspond to 0730's prespecified analysis
- [ ] Causal language is limited to the design's population, intervention and assumptions
- [ ] Null/adverse results remain publishable; loss of identification returns to the author
